<article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $\calD $ be the full subcategory of $\Fun (\Delta ^1,\calC )$ spanned by the elements of $S$, and form a fiber diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { \calD _{X} \ar [r] \ar [d] &amp;  \calD \ar [d] \\ \{ X\}  \ar [r] &amp;  \Fun (\{ 0\} , \calC )} \]
</div>
    <p>
       Since $S$ is stable under pushouts, the right vertical map is a coCartesian fibration, so that the above diagram is homotopy Cartesian by Proposition <a href="/tag/CAB1EC" data-tag="CAB1EC">3.3.1.3</a>. Lemma <a href="/tag/A2B834" data-tag="A2B834">5.5.5.9</a> asserts that $\calD $ is accessible, so that $\calD _{X}$ is accessible by Proposition <a href="/tag/950E9C" data-tag="950E9C">5.4.6.6</a>. Using Lemma <a href="/tag/806806" data-tag="806806">5.5.5.11</a>, we conclude that $\calD _{X}$ is presentable, so that $\calD _{X}$ has a final object $f: X \rightarrow Y$. To complete the proof, it will suffice to show that $Y$ is $S$-local. 
    <p>
      Let $t: A \rightarrow B$ be an arbitrary morphism in $\calC $ which belongs to $S$. We wish to show that composition with $t$ induces a homotopy equivalence $\phi : \bHom _{\calC }(B,Y) \rightarrow \bHom _{\calC }(A,Y)$. Let $g: A \rightarrow Y$ be an arbitrary morphism; using Lemma <a href="/tag/461171" data-tag="461171">5.5.5.12</a> we may identify $\bHom _{\calC _{A/}}(t,g)$ with the homotopy fiber of $\phi $ over the base point $g$ of $\bHom _{\calC }(A,Y)$. We wish to show that this space is contractible. Form a pushout diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { A \ar [d]^{g} \ar [r]^{t} &amp;  B \ar [d] \\ Y \ar [r]^{t'} &amp;  Z} \]
</div>
    <p>
       in the $\infty $-category $\calC $. Lemma <a href="/tag/060EE1" data-tag="060EE1">5.5.5.13</a> implies the existence of a homotopy equivalence $\bHom _{\calC _{A/}}(t,g) \simeq \bHom _{\calC _{Y/}}(t', \id _{Y})$. It will therefore suffice to prove that $\bHom _{\calC _{Y/}}(t', \id _{Y})$ is contractible. Since $t'$ is a pushout of $t$, it belongs to $S$. Let $\sigma $ be a $2$-simplex of $\calC $ classifying a diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { &amp;  Y \ar [dr]^{t'} &amp;  \\ X \ar [ur]^{s} \ar [rr]^{s'} &amp;  &amp;  Z, } \]
</div>
    <p>
       so that $s'$ is a composition of the morphisms $s$ and $t'$ in $\calC $, and therefore also belongs to $S$. Applying Lemma <a href="/tag/461171" data-tag="461171">5.5.5.12</a> again, we may identify 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[ \bHom _{\calC _{Y/}}(t', \id _{C'}) \simeq \bHom _{\calC _{s/}}(\sigma , s_1(s)) \]
</div>
    <p>
       with the homotopy fiber of the map 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \bHom _{ \calC _{Y/}}(s',s) \rightarrow \bHom _{ \calC _{Y/}}(s,s). \]
</div>
    <p>
       given by composition with $\sigma $. By construction, $\calD _{X}$ is a full subcategory of $\calC ^{X/}$ which contains $s$ and $s'$, and $s$ is a final object of $\calD _{X}$. In view of the equivalence of $\calC _{X/}$ with $\calC ^{X/}$, we conclude that the spaces $\bHom _{\calC _{X/}}(s',s)$ and $\bHom _{\calC _{X/}}(s,s)$ are contractible, so that $\phi $ is a homotopy equivalence as desired. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
